[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     19[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Mismatches found and fixed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 1. Exit status differed when stdout is a pipe with no reader (SIGPIPE)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom.** With stdin `abc\nz\n` and stdout connected to a pipe whose read end
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m is already closed:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | | stdout | stderr | status |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---|---|---|---|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | C | (none) | (empty) | killed by signal 13 (`SIGPIPE`) |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Rust (before fix) | (none) | (empty) | exit 0 |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** The Rust standard library sets `SIGPIPE` to `SIG_IGN` before `main`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m runs. The failing write therefore returned `EPIPE`, which the translation
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m discarded (`let _ = write!(...)`), and the process exited 0. The C program keeps
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the default disposition, so the same write raises `SIGPIPE` and the process is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m killed. `printf` output is buffered, so in C the fatal write is the flush at
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `exit`, but the observable result is the same signal death.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** `restore_default_sigpipe()` in `translation/src/main.rs` calls
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `signal(SIGPIPE, SIG_DFL)` as the first statement of `main`, restoring the C
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m disposition. No change to the writing code was needed.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Regression test.** `closed_stdout_is_fatal_via_sigpipe` builds a pipe, closes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the read end, hands the write end to each child as stdout, and requires both to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m report signal 13. The test also asserts the C side really is signal 13, so the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m test cannot silently degrade into a tautology if the C behaviour ever changes.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m This was the only observable divergence found. Everything below was verified as
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m already matching, and is covered by tests so it stays that way.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Behaviours confirmed identical (including the ones that look like bugs)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m These are the branch points of the C program. There are no `if` statements in
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `main`; every branch lives inside `fgets`, `strlen` and `strcspn`, so the input
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m classes were enumerated from those.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`fgets` returns NULL at EOF.** The buffers are `char s1[100] = ""`, so a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   failed `fgets` leaves the previous contents — here, all zeros. Empty stdin
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   therefore prints `0`. Covered by `empty_input`, `stdin_at_eof_from_dev_null`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   and `single_line_with_newline` (second `fgets` fails).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`fgets` keeps the trailing newline** and stops after at most 99 bytes.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   A 99-byte line leaves no newline in the buffer, so the rest of the physical
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   line is what the *second* `fgets` reads: `s2` can come from the same input
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   line as `s1`. Covered by `fgets_length_boundary`, `full_length_sweep`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   (every line-1 length 0..=205 crossed with newline presence and five shapes of
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   line 2) and `match_position_across_the_boundary`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`s[strlen(s) - 1] = '\0'` is unconditional.** For a normal line this strips
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   the newline. When the line was truncated at 99 bytes or ended at EOF without a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   newline, it strips a **data** byte instead. This is replicated, not fixed:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `single_line_without_newline`, `second_line_without_newline` and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `chop_removes_the_matching_char` (the chop removes the byte that would
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   otherwise have matched, changing the printed number).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`s[strlen(s) - 1]` when `strlen(s) == 0` writes out of bounds** at `s[-1]`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   This happens on empty stdin and whenever a line's first byte is NUL. The write
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   is unobservable in this program: `fgets(s, 100, ...)` can only ever place a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   NUL, never a data byte, at index 99 of either array, and both arrays start
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   zeroed, so the byte preceding either buffer is already 0 whichever order the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   compiler lays the two arrays out in. The translation skips the write and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   documents why. Verified empirically to produce identical output in
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `empty_input`, `nul_at_start_of_each_line`, `nul_sweep_over_offsets` and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `nul_only_input`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Embedded NUL bytes are stored by `fgets` but terminate the C string.**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `ab\0cd` has `strlen` 2, so `strcspn` only ever sees `ab` — and after the chop,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `a`. Covered by `nul_sweep_over_offsets` (NUL at every offset 0..=101) and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `nul_only_input`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`strcspn` set semantics.** The reject set is the bytes of `s2` excluding its
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   terminator; the scan stops at `s1`'s terminator; an empty `s2` yields
